<Project>
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Give every test host a thread pool that does not have to grow.

      A test that drives a FakeTimeProvider and then awaits a signal the component
      under test raises is deterministic in the only sense that matters: it waits
      for the work, not for a duration. What it still depends on is the runtime
      scheduling the continuation that carries that signal. Above the pool's
      minimum, threads are injected at a throttled rate rather than on demand, so
      on a machine where the ready threads are already busy a continuation can sit
      queued for seconds. The wait bounding the failure then expires and the test
      reports a defect in the subject that is not there.

      The minimum defaults to the processor count, which is small on a CI runner
      and large here, which is why this only ever shows up in CI: see issue #330,
      where two tests in unrelated assemblies failed on consecutive
      `test (windows-latest)` runs of one commit while ubuntu passed both times,
      and neither reproduced across three full local suite runs on 24 cores.

      This is a mitigation, not a proven fix. It removes injection delay as a
      cause; it does not prove injection delay was the cause. The failure has not
      been reproduced outside CI, and the timeout messages in
      OpenAlAudioSinkFakeDeviceTests and ClockSelectVideoSinkTests now print pool
      state so the next occurrence says whether the pool was starved.

      Test hosts only. Nothing under src/ inherits this, so it cannot mask a
      starvation problem in shipped code.
    -->
    <ThreadPoolMinThreads>32</ThreadPoolMinThreads>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
